removal performance of all these chemicals in the water. Parts of these contaminants in drinking water have already been treated as the first level of control in the contaminants candidate list (CCL) proposed by the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) in the USA [9]. How to extract and remove these pollutants in the water environment has become one of the significant issues in the field of Advanced water treatment [1, 10, 11].
Much research has focused on advanced water purification technologies for water decontamination since the 20th century (Fig. 1). The conventional technologies include biodegradation, adsorption, electrochemical, photocatalysis, and chemical oxidations (ozonation and chemical Fenton reaction) or their combinations [11–24]. Among them, the advanced oxidation process (AOP) has great advantages in destroying refractory organic compounds completely instead of the physical separation because of the formation of diverse reactive oxygen species (ROS), including the hydroxyl radicals (·OH) with an oxidation potential of 1.8–2.7 V and sulfate radical (SO4-) with an oxidation potential of 2.5–3.1 V [19, 20, 25–29]. As shown in Fig. 1, the Fenton reaction was invented by the researcher Fenton in the year 1894. The oxidation of tartaric acid was observed by the combination of ferrous ions \((\mathrm{Fe}^{2+})\) and hydrogen peroxide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\) . Until the 1960s, the Fenton reaction was widely used in degrading organic
pollutants. In 1964, the reaction constants between the nonselective \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) and common organic contaminants in water were calculated systematically [30]. Based on these fundamental investigations, Glaze et al. precisely defined AOPs for the first time in 1987 (Fig. 1) [4]. In 1982, the classical peroxone process was identified by combining ozone and hydrogen peroxide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\) [31]. In the following decades, the photo-Fenton and electrophoto methods [32–34] using the additional energy of electricity and light were investigated in 1992 and 2001 in advanced water treatment.
One of the challenging issues in limiting the further application of these technologies is the important chemical requirement or energy consumption in light or electricity [10]. Other studies to overcome the limitation of high-energy consumption are crucial for applying AOPs in the full-scale water treatment project [35].
To avoid the fundamental as well as the practical challenges of AOPs, engineered nanomaterials (ENMs) as heterogeneous catalysts arouse great research interest. These materials have several unique properties [22, 36–38], including particle sizes, electron configuration at the atomic scale [10, 39–53], and the tunable physical/chemical properties or electrical/optical properties, making them promising alternatives for the integration of the modular system with small scale.
Some pioneering work reports MOFs from Yaghi and Kitagawa's research group [54–56]. As shown in Fig. 1, the crystalline porous material has unique chemical/physical properties, such as the high surface area, averaged separated metal sites, and tunable porous channels [57, 58]. A large amount of work has been conducted using MOFs in the field of separation, catalysis, adsorption, or energy storage [59–62]. Xu et al.'s research group developed the metal-organic framework (MOF) derived
carbon in 2008 [63], showing good electrochemical properties in an electrochemical double-layered capacitor. The center metal could serve as the active site for the catalytic properties. In the year of 2011, the concept of single-atom catalysis (SACs) by single-atom Pt site was reported by Zhang's group [64], and since then, increasing work has been conducted by using SAs [65–71]. However, when the size of the metal particles is decreased to the single atom (SA) scale, the surface area of the single atom will increase, resulting in an increase in the free energy of the metal surface. Under this circumstance, agglomeration could occur to form a large cluster. To avoid this disadvantage, in 2015, Jaouen F. et al. used MOFs as a single atom carrier in the area of oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) [72]. Within the framework of MOFs derived material, the reaction sites were identified. The effectiveness of this heterogeneous catalytic reaction is greatly improved by employing the materials of MOFs [72]. In 2019, Jiang's group summarized the MOFs-based single atom for catalysis, discussing the SAs from MOFs and their derived carbons [73]. Recent years, increasing number of important studied has been conducted on the area of designing single atoms and their applications. For example, works focus on the electrocatalysts [74–76], the regulation of local atomic environment for the production of hydrogen peroxide [77], electronic devices [77, 78], and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction [79, 80].
Several novel studies have shown that SAs can degrade water pollutants selectively and effectively since 2017 (Fig. 1). In the photo-Fenton reaction, peroxymonosulfate (PMS) can be decomposed by single atom \(\mathrm{Fe-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) [81]. A cobalt-based single atom was designed to activate PMS in 2018 [82]. In 2020, a Cu-single fraction in the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) carrier showed great potential in a Fenton filter [35].
Metal-organic framework derived carbon-based single atom is a novel material to fabricate the single active sites in carbon materials. Most recent research focuses on energy-related catalysis [83–85]. Although limited studies have been reported in advanced water treatment [81, 86–91], the reported SAs have shown great potential in contaminant purification. In this review, the synthesized method of SAs derived from MOFs-based carbon has been systematically discussed. Advanced characterization technologies for the identification of SAs have been introduced. The reported SAs materials and metal-derived porous carbon materials for the advanced oxidation process have been summarized and compared. Finally, some further perspective on the existing research topic is discussed.
## 2 General principles for SA from MOFs
In this section, only the carbonization of MOFs was discussed despite some reports confirming that SAs can be formed using pristine MOFs [92]. Recently, the reported SAs derived from MOFs are mostly N-containing porous carbon-based material. Several advantages are summarized in fabricating SAs from MOFs derived materials. (i) The MOFs derived carbon retain the porous channels, which could facilitate the mass transfer process and reduce the diffusion resistance; (ii) MOFs derived carbon effectively have a tunable topological structure, and high surface area inherited from pristine MOFs; (iii) single atoms are firmly bonded by the coordination bonds, which could decrease the possibility of aggregation toward clusters and nanoparticles; (iv) conductivity properties can be improved in the process of pyrolysis.
Recent studies have reported several strategies to synthesize MOF-derived carbon-based single atoms (Fig. 2). Metal-organic
frameworks were synthesized via a self-assembly process, within which the various metal centers and organic ligands coordinate together. The organic linkers and metal atoms could anchor together within the framework of carbon materials. The stability and catalytic performance can be improved by changing the electron configuration and adjustable skeleton. Simple heat treatment under high temperatures can effectively convert MOFs to SAs. This facile heat treatment is feasible in making effective catalysts based on the MOFs as precursors in several applications. One of the advantages of the single or mixed metal strategy is facile to disperse target metal atoms in MOFs. In this strategy, the Zn-based bimetallic MOFs are commonly used, for example, nitrogen-rich zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are served as a promising sacrificial precursor. More types of MOFs should be studied as the precursor. Some weaknesses are existed in the pore confinement strategy, for example, the metal clusters with sizes larger than the windows of MOFs are not suitable to be encapsulated in the pores. Detailed methods are discussed in this section.
## 2.1 Single/mixed metal strategy
Single metal strategies are previously used to generate the SAs [93]. Based on the current fundamental research, the SAs can be feasibly obtained using direct heat treatment. Metals inside the structure can transfer into the carbon support at high temperatures. As shown in Fig. 2(a)(ii), Jiang et al. used ZIF-8 containing N and Zn in the framework as the precursor. They reported a novel strategy to synthesize the hollow porous carbon featuring averaged separated nitrogen and single Zn atoms [93]. The ZIF-8 grows on the out surface of the –COOH–modified polystyrene (PS) particles. Then N, N-dimethyl formamide
solution was used to remove the PS core, leading to the hollow nitrogen-contained ZIF-8 spheres. Finally, the single Zn atom with extremely high loading (11.3 wt.%) can be obtained by the carbonization of this ZIF-8 at \(800\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\). ZIF-8 was commonly selected as the potential precursor to fabricate SAs because of the N element's high loading [93, 99]. The synthesized material with hierarchical pores can catalytically fix \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) using the photothermal effect. Other similar studies obtained the single Ni atom using the direct carbonization of pristine MOFs. Different from the Zn center, an additional acid leaching procedure is needed since Ni's higher evaporation temperature and NP's formation during this process [100].
Zn-centered MOFs are commonly used in mixed metal strategies (Fig. 2(a)(ii)) since their properties of the self-evaporation process (melted point of \(420\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) boiling and point of \(907\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\)) during the pyrolysis process. Metal in the framework of MOFs can be easily replaced by other metal ions, which makes it possible to introduce the target metal within the framework. \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\), etc., were usually selected to replace \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) within the structure because these metals have a similar size to \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) [94]. These target metals are highly dispersed in the system. During the pyrolysis process, the N-containing linkers are transferred into the N-doped carbon, on which the single metal atoms are immobilized.
On the other hand, the hierarchical porous carbon support was generated after the Zn species evaporated, which is beneficial for the mass transport and catalytic process [12, 94]. Co-doped ZIF-8 can be used as the precursor of Co-SAs. In 2016, the mixed-metal strategy was applied to synthesize the Co SAs [94]. The bimetallic \(\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Co}\) MOFs were predesigned. Co atom was reduced to a single atom during the carbonization process of the organic ligand of MOFs, and Zn was evaporated (Fig. 2(a)(ii)). The homogeneous distribution of two metals in MOFs can be designed. Other SAs can also be fabricated using this strategy. For example, the \(\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) species can be obtained from the thermal treatment of \(\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) modified ZIF-8 [90]. Ni SAs have been obtained by the carbonization of the Ni-ZIF-8 in the argon environment [101]. It is to be noted that the heating temperature will influence the electron configuration. For example, the coordination environment of Co sites is proved to be \(\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) at the temperature of 1000 and \(800\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\), respectively [94].
## 2.2 Metal-containing linker strategy
The organic linker of MOFs can be functionalized by doping with foreign metal atoms. Some ligands, including porphyrin, \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), dipyridyl, etc., can be designed as chelation sites for loading the metal cations. Selecting suitable linkers with heteroatom could form a strong interaction and generate a reasonable distance between each metal in the framework. For example, the Zr-porphyrin-based MOFs with mixed linkers include \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}\) (TCPP = tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin) and Fe-TCPP. Fe-SAs immobilized on carbon support were obtained after carbonization and acid etching procedures [95]. Fe-SA-N-C can be proved by forming \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) configuration, which presents excellent catalytic performance (Fig. 2(b)(ii)). For the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), another representative case was the metal coordinated with free \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) groups on \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) (terephthalic acid) linkers [102]. The Ru-SAs-C catalyst retains the original framework of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UIO-66 and has the Ru SAs. In this way, the aggregation of Ru atoms to Ru NPs during the heating treatment can be avoided.
## 2.3 Pore confinement strategy
The other effective method to introduce the SAs based on MOFs could be the pyrolysis of the MOFs-based composites [96]. MOFs
contain tunable porous structures, making an ideal platform to encapsulate the guest components within the pores. Double solvents methods (DSMs) are one of the effective procedures to obtain a uniform distribution of metal ions (Fig. 2(b)(i)). For example, \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) species and dicyandiamide were capsulated into the pores of the \(\mathrm{MIL}-101-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). The composite was hydrolyzed to form a graphitic carbon with hierarchical channels. \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}\) can be encapsulated into the pores of ZIF-8 (1.16 nm) since the smaller sizes of \(0.97\ \mathrm{nm}\) [96]. Because of the spatial confinement process, one pore can only capture one Fe-containing molecular. The N-containing carbon was derived from the organic linkers of MOFs. As a result, the Fe SAs were generated after the pyrolysis and acid treatment.
Another MOF (MET series) can be used as the potential platform to introduce the SAs. For example, the MET-6 series contains Zn-triazole-rich linkers, and the Co and Fe species can be reduced to SA after the carbonization process [103]. The Zn species were evaporated under high temperatures, and the hierarchical porous structures were generated.
## 2.4 Other strategies
There are other methods to synthesize MOFs derived SAs besides the above methods of the direct pyrolysis method (Fig. 2(c)(i)). For example, the gas-migration method is one of the practical processes for fabricating the SAs. Under the heat treatment, the target metal was vaporized and transferred to the metal vacancies of the MOFs in the protective gas atmosphere. A series of SAs were obtained using the thermal diffusion strategy, including Co, Ni, and Cu [97, 104]. For example, fentanyl Cu and ZIF-8 were selected as the support, and the Cu-SAs can be obtained by using the ammonia molecule acting as the medium to expel the Cu atom. Then the Cu atom was anchored onto the Zn center of ZIF-8 [104]. The as-prepared dicyandiamide and Bi-MOF use the solvent-thermal method. Bi-\(\mathrm{N}_{4}\) structure was fabricated successfully [105]. In the process, the dicyandiamide was transferred into ammonia gas during the heating treatment. Then the ammonia gas pulled out the metal. Then the N-rich porous carbon, derived from the organic linkers, captured the metal. Another method to make SAs are the pyrolysis MOFs with bulk metal, which is a top-down strategy [98]. The loaded metal nanoparticles can transfer into a single atom in this strategy (Figs. 2(d)(i) and 2(e)(i)). For example, Pd SAs were obtained by the thermally atomized process from the Pd-NP @ZIF-8 [106]. Fe SAs-N/C was separated by the acid treatment in which the iron oxide was removed [107].
## 3 Characterization method
As discussed above, the precursor's organic linker with high N loading offers abundant coordination sites for single chelating atoms. The organic linkers can be carbonized into CN material with porosity during heat treatment. As a result, stable \(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{N}_{x}\) bonds can be formed [73, 108, 109]. The strong interaction firmly anchored the averaged separated atoms onto the carbon support. Studies show that the metal complex on the carbon support serves as the active center for a series of catalytic reactions. Identifying the active center with adjustable atomic configuration is crucial to explore the catalytical mechanism of the chemical reactions. The active sites of single metals within the framework can be identified using advanced characterization methods, including spectroscopic techniques such as the X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) and the electron microscopy method (Figs. 3–5), the aberration-corrected high angle angular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM), XAFS measurement, infrared (IR) spectroscopy, and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy.
## 3.1 Electron microscopy
Increasing methods have been applied to identify the microstructure of the MOFs derived carbon material and its derived SAs. Electron microscopy is one of the most commonly used tools to understand their properties [110]. Based on the aberration-corrected high angle angular dark field-scanning transmission electron microscopy, the structure at the atomic level can be observed. Because of the different \(Z\) values of metal single atom and atom on the support materials, the SAs can be easily distinguished. This is one of the direct methods to confirm the existence of the single atom instead of the metal cluster or the nanoparticles. For example, Fe-doped ZIF-8 was applied as the MOFs-based precursor to fabricate the well-dispersed atomic Fe sites on the carbon support under the pyrolysis process (Fig. 3(a)). The graphitic \(_\mathrm{N}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe-N_{4}}\) structures were generated under increasing temperatures. The electron microscopy method can observe the particle sizes of the Fe SAs derived from ZIF-8 (Fig. 3(b)). The dodecahedron crystal of ZIF can be adjustable, ranging from 200 to \(1000\ \mathrm{nm}\) [111]. The aberration-corrected HAADFSTEM image was used to describe the atomic structure. As can be seen in Fig. 3(c), atomic Fe sites are located in the red dot. Electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) was used to further confirm this spot is Fe species (Fig. 3(d)). The atomic dispersed Fe atom in the support could increase the active sites' density, possibly increasing the catalytic performance [85]. As shown in Fig. 3(e), a cobalt-doped carbon (Co–N–C) was derived from the ZIF-8 precursor by mixed metal strategy [85]. A two-step encapsulation and linkers exchange process was used to fabricate the \(\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}\) site in the support. Figures 3(f) and \(3(\mathrm{g})\) represent the STEM of the as-synthesized materials. From the image, the dodecahedron crystal of ZIF-based materials can be obtained. Aberration-corrected HAADF-STEM image of cobalt-doped carbon (Co–N–C) derived from ZIF-8 at the scale of \(2\;\mathrm{nm}\) can be obtained from Figs. 3(h) and 3(i), from which the formation of single atom Co can be verified. Figures 3(j)–3(n) present the first study to fabricate the MOF-derived carbon by perfluoroalkoxy (PFA)/MOF-5 composite carbonization [63]. Figures 3(j)–3(n) show the synthesized procedure, the scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images, the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern, and the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherms, respectively. The as-prepared nanoporous materials show a highly developed hierarchical porosity in the framework and a high hydrogen storage capacity.
