## 3.2 XAFS methods
Research on the relationship between the atomic structure and the activity is crucial for understanding the reaction mechanism and
rational design of the desired material with satisfied performance. XAFS spectroscopy measurement is one of the most commonly used methods to fabricate the SAs derived from a metal-organic framework [112]. This technique includes the X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) and the extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectroscopies. Using this process, the atomic dispersion of the SAs and the oxidation state, as well as the coordination numbers or configuration, can be obtained by analyzing the spectrum [64, 107]. An increasing number of studies discuss the reaction mechanism and the relationships between the structure–activity using this technology [113–115]. For example, as shown in Fig. 4, Fe and phthalocyanine (FePc) was encapsulated in the pores of ZIF-8. The pyrolysis of these predesigned composites obtained the FePc-x@ZIF-8 structure, as shown in Fig. 4(a) [107]. The TEM and HRTEM show that the N-doped porous carbons (NPCs) were generated during the pyrolysis process (Figs. 4(b) and 4(c)). Atomically dispersed Fe-N site can be obtained by reducing the Fe from the trapped FePc composites at the mesoporous support. Fe atoms can be identified by observing the HAADF-STEM image of the as-synthesized Fe SAs-N/C (Fig. 4(d)). X-ray absorption spectroscopy was performed to unravel the Fe-N site's chemical state and coordination number [107]. Fe K-edge XANES, the Fourier-transformed (FT) \( k^{3} \)-weighted EXAFS spectra, and the FT-EXAFS fitting curves, respectively, of the as-synthesized Fe SAs-N/C derived from MOFs, are shown in Figs. 4(e)–4(g), respectively.
Figure 4(e) (Fe K-edge, XANES) shows that the valence state of Fe in the Fe-SA-N/C material is between Fe(II) and Fe(III). Figures 4(f) and 4(g) (FT \( k^{3} \)-weighted EXAFS spectra) show that the Fe atom was coordinated with four N atoms. The Fe-N\(_{4}\) moiety structure was proved by using the \( k^{3} \)-weighted EXAFS spectra [107].
## 3.3 Other techniques
Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is usually used to analyze the chemical structure. IR spectra can identify the chemical structure and functional groups. The IR spectra of the substance contained the fingerprint features. The in situ infrared technology is also effective in catalysis [116]. The hydrocarbons produced during the reaction can be detected using single-atom catalysts. The characteristic peak of target compounds can be identified before and after the chemical reaction. The interactions between the single atom \( \mathrm{Sn}^{k+} \) and \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) were studied by analyzing the IR spectrum [117, 118]. Other methods to study the reaction mechanism are X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra and in situ diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) [119]. The catalytic process can be studied by analyzing the intermediates. The NMR technique was applied in the previous study. For example, the solid-state magic-angle spinning NMR was used to study the metal species and organic linkers of Pt/Au catalysis [120, 121]. The coordinately unsaturated sites of \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \) were found to be anchor site for Pt-SA through the interaction of oxygen bridges [120].
## 4 MOFs derived carbon for water treatment
MOFs-based carbon shows great potential in environmental adsorption and catalysis because of the tunable morphology, large surface area, and rich active metal sites within the framework. Previous studies found that metal-organic framework-derived carbon has several advantages in the area of advanced water treatment: (a) Studies show that the MOFs derived carbon material can retain the original framework with high surface area and good stability in a water environment; (b) the adjustable active metal sites can be constructed by the predesigned different metal center in the pristine framework of MOFs; (c) the regularly arranged metal ions in the MOFs crystalline are beneficial to avoid the aggregation of the metal/metal oxide nanoparticles and tend to be relatively ordered separated; (d) the hierarchical porous structures can be designed which has the advantage to increase the mass transfer process in water. Some recent work on the application of MOFs derived from carbon as summarized.
On the other hand, the MOFs derived carbon with active metal sites can be classified as follows: the metal-free carbon materials, the metal/metal oxide materials, and heteroatom (nitrogen, sulfur, or phosphorus) doped material. Zn-containing MOFs are suitable for metal-free materials because of their lower boiling point. The Zn species can evaporate at higher temperatures and leave the desired vacancies. For example, ZIF-8, MAF-5, and MAF-6 can be used as the Zn-containing precursors. The obtained surface ranges from 409 to \( 1466~\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1} \). Another strategy to remove the metal/metal oxide-free carbon material is the acid treatment (HCl or hydrofluoric acid (Hf)). After washing with HF \( (20\%) \) and deionized water, the surface area \( (\sim1490\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}) \) and N content \( (\sim 9.4\,\mathrm{wt.}\%) \) were significantly improved.
Other MOFs with different metal centers (Co, Ni, or Fe) were studied previously to form the metal/metal oxide containing carbon material. The metal oxide and metal nanoparticles can be produced during this process depending on the reduction potential. Co-MOFs MOFs have aroused great attention in recent
years. Some Co nanoparticles and Co oxide were formed during the pyrolysis process. For example, Co-CoO@porous carbon materials were produced with some percentage of CoO contents \( (\sim\,7\,\mathrm{\wt.}\%) \) in the structure. The selection of metal ions in the precursor can avoid the aggregation of the metal ions during the heat treatment. Changing different metals in the MOF-based precursor can adjust the various dispersed metal sites in the carbon materials. Besides, previous studies show that the other nonmetallic heteroatoms, including the N, P, and D, can effectively adjust the properties of the target carbon materials. For example, adding melamine into a ZIF-8-based precursor shows a higher surface area \( (\sim\ 1421\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}) \) and a higher degree of graphitization. The phosphorus or sulfur in the precursor can achieve better performance in the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) process. The obtained materials can be applied in various areas, such as photocatalytic degradation or electrochemical reaction.
## 4.1 Photocatalytic degradation
As shown in Fig. 5, the MOF-derived carbon can be applied in the photocatalytic degradation of pollutants in water [122]. Previous pioneer work has been done in which MOFs were used as the precursors. Cao et al. designed three-dimensional graphene networks (3DGNs) using ZIF-8 and MIL-88-Fe as the precursors [123]. The \( \mathrm{ZnO}/3\mathrm{DGN} \) and \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/3\mathrm{DGN} \) composites were synthesized. Figure 5(a) presents the SEM image of the novel material. MOFs were successfully grown onto the surface of 3DGN. After this composite was prepared, the composites were annealed under argon gas. Nanoparticles with a size of around \( 10\,\mathrm{\nm} \) were obtained. The size of the crystals was adjustable, ranging from 100 to \( 900~\mathrm{\nm} \), by changing the synthesized conditions. Since \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) was an efficient photocatalyst with low cost, methylene blue degradation was performed under ultraviolet (UV) light irradiation [123]. The degradation of methylene blue under UV irradiation within \( 60\;\mathrm{min} \) can be observed in Fig. 5(b). A synergistic effect can be seen when the novel MOFs derive metal
oxide as the photocatalyst. This organic pollutant's complete degradation \((>90\%)\) can be observed in the water much faster than the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) and 3DGN [123]. As shown in Fig. 5(c), a novel core–shell photocatalyst derived from MOFs (ZIF-8) was successfully designed [124]. In this work, the hollow \(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co}\) matrix consists of a ZIF-8 shell and some Co-ZIFs inside the pores. The structure presents a high surface area and adsorption capacities for the target compounds. Methyl orange can be effectively degraded in the photocatalytic degradation process. Figure 5(d) shows these novel materials' plausible reaction pathways to degrade the organic pollutants in water. Results show that the graphitized carbon contains many electronic traps, making the electron-transfer process to the surface easy. \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) radicals can be produced from oxygen in the system, effectively degrading water pollutants. On the other hand, the holes would oxidize the water to \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\), which can degrade the organic compounds effectively [124].
## 4.2 Electrocatalytic degradation
Another representative study using MOFs-derived carbon in the area in the electrochemical-related process was conducted by Jiang and his co-workers in 2015 (Fig. 6(a)). In this study, the bimetallic ZIFs (BMZIFs) derived from two commonly used MOFs, ZIF-67 and ZIF-8, were successfully synthesized [125]. The predesigned MOFs were carbonized under \(900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the next step. Then the MOFs-derived carbon was successfully fabricated. The novel porous carbon was proved to have a high surface area and degree of graphitization. The hierarchical porous structure and \(\mathrm{CoN}_{x}\) sites can serve as effective active sites for the catalytic reaction. Recently, Huang et al. rationally designed and fabricated a series of copperMOFs derived carbon electrodes [125]. The copper oxide carbon materials \(\left(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}{-}\mathrm{C}\right)\) have a similar chemical structure to Cu-BTC.
The pristine MOFs and their derived carbon material have hierarchical pores, including mesoporous and macroporous structures. As seen in Fig. 6(b), \(\mathrm{CuO}_{x}\)-derived carbon proved to be a promising material to degrade the antibiotics in the electrochemical process [126]. \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) should be the active species for degrading water pollutants in water, which is proved by the
quenching experiment in Fig. 6(b). Results show that the degradation efficiency of cephalosporin is much higher than other water pollutants. The high selectivity of the \(\upbeta\)-lactam antibiotics can be observed in this novel electrode. The \(\mathrm{Cu}(0)\) on the carbon surface can form \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) through electron transfer. The complexation of \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and the \(\upbeta\)-lactam could facilitate the degradation process. Thus this electrochemical degradation is selective toward a specific group of antibiotics [126].
## 5 Single-atom catalysts for advanced water treatment
Compared with the MOF-derived carbon discussed above, recently, the MOF-derived carbon-based single atom has been extensively studied. Their application in advanced water treatment arouses great research interests because of their maximum atomic utilization and ultrahigh catalytic properties in various research areas. Compared to the MOFs-derived nanocluster or the traditional nanoparticles, this special electron configuration of SAs will facilitate the formation of a large amount of ROS, including the \(\cdot\mathrm{O}^{2-}\), \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-}\), \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\), and \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [35, 81, 87, 89, 114, 115, 127, 128]. The coordination bond between the metallic and nonmetallic atoms in the framework could also show a better performance of water stability. A series of research found excellent catalytic performance in degrading various water pollutants in recent years [35, 81, 87, 89, 114, 115, 127, 128]. On the other hand, the reaction mechanism between the catalytic behavior and the active sites at the atomic level could be explained by analyzing the SAs based on the advanced characterization methods discussed in the previous section [73, 86, 108]. In this review section, several recent studies of the application of the single atom in water treatment have been discussed. Table 1 lists representative MOFs derived from metal oxide and single-atom catalysts and their performance in the advanced water treatment process in recent studies. Although the application of this novel SA is still limited, the great advantages of the SAs in the advanced area of water treatment with the desired structures have already been discovered.
## 5.1 SAs in photo-Fenton process
The photocatalysis process is crucial for water treatment. Several recent studies focus on designing novel material with SA for the degradation of micropollutants (e.g., bisphenol A (BPA), pharmaceuticals, and personal care products) in water under visible light irradiation. For example, using a molecular engineering strategy, the single atom sites can be designed with adjustable transition metal centers, including the Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, and Cu. A heterogeneous Fenton-like process (HFLP) has inherent disadvantages for water treatment [81]. For example, the \(\mathrm{Fe(II)}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe(III)}+\mathrm{OH}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{Fe(III)}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe(II)}+\mathrm{HO}_{2}^{-}+\mathrm{H}^{+}\), the second step is usually considered as the rate-limiting step in the catalytic process because the second-order constant between the Fe III and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is \(0.001{-}0.01\ \mathrm{M^{-1}{\cdot}S^{-1}}\) [81]. The redox cycle of \(\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}\) is crucial to determine the reaction efficiency of the HELP. As shown in Fig. 7(a), single-atom with different metal sites, including Fe, Mn, Co, Cr, and Cu, were successfully synthesized by Chen et al. [81]. The pyrrolic N-rich graphitic carbon nitride \((\mathrm{PN-g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})\) was the carbon support to fabricate the transition metal sites at a large scale. The specific procedure for the scalable production of SA can be found in Fig. 7(a). A series of advanced characterization methods, including the HAADF-STEM, HRTEM, and Cr K-edge XANES, high-resolution N 1s XPS, etc., have been used to describe the surface properties and chemical structure at the atomic scale. Over \(98\%\) of the BPA can be degraded during the process of HFLR at a wide \(\mathrm{pH}\) range from 3 to 11 in water. The BPA can be effectively degraded in visible-light-induced photo-Fenton with the optima \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) dosage of \(163\;\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}\). The proposed mechanism is consistent with similar photo-Fenton reactions without forming SA (Fig. 7(b)). The SAs catalyst was activated under the irradiation of visible light. Oxygen can be reduced to \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\)while \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) can be produced by activating \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and oxidation of \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)ions. The reaction of \(\equiv\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\equiv\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}\) can be proved by analyzing XPS spectra. Both the oxidation and regeneration of the \(\equiv\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}\) occurred during the photo-Fenton system [81].
Another Fe-SAs photocatalyst was fabricated by Zhan's research group (Fig. 7(c)). A defect strategy was applied to design the iron single-atom photocatalyst with the structure of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\)-\(\mathrm{N}_{3}/\mathrm{CN}\) [128]. A large amount of nitrogen vacancies were proved possible active sites for the activation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in photo-Fenton. One of the representative pharmaceuticals and personal care products, ciprofloxacin, was selected as the model compound to verify the effectiveness of this novel material. The structure of \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{N}_{4}\) sites and nitrogen vacancy of the \(\mathrm{N}_{3\mathrm{c}}\) type was proved by analyzing the EXAFS and XPS analysis. A series of characterization technologies, including the enhanced permeability and retention effect (EPR) spectra and HAADF-STEM, were applied to identify the active sites within the framework. As shown in Fig. 7(d), ciprofloxacin can rapidly degrade within \(60\;\mathrm{\min}\) during the visible-light photo-Fenton process [128]. In comparison, the degradation of ciprofloxacin in the processes of chemical Fenton process \((\mathrm{Cat}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})\), photocatalysis (Cat/Vis), and the reference group \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) or the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{Vis})\) without the SAs were not obvious. It is noted that during this study, the uniformly delocalized orbits of the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of the SA-Fe1-N4/CN can improve the photo-Fenton process [128].
## 5.2 SAs in PMS/PDS activation in water treatment
Several recent studies prove that the metal SA catalysts are quite effective in the activation of PMS oxidation \(\left(\mathrm{HSO}_{5}^{-}\right)\)and peroxydisulfate (PDS oxidation, \(\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}^{2-}\)) in the advanced oxidation process for water treatment [113, 114, 129]. SA-Fe-NC was proven effective in catalysis for the activation of PMS/PDS. A novel iron-based catalyst was synthesized by Hu's research group [88] (Figs. 8(a) and 8(b)). During this study, the SA-Fe-NC was first fabricated by the carbonization of sodium citrate at \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The ferric nitrate and glucose were used to obtain the Fe complex. The SA can be obtained by heat treatment with melamine in an Ar atmosphere at \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). HADDF-STEM images and FT of Fe K-
edge EXAFS spectra of the SA of Fe proved the successful synthesis of the single atom of Fe on the support. The above analysis confirmed Fe-pyridinic \(\mathrm{N}_{4}\) and Fe-pyrrolic \(\mathrm{N}_{4}\) configurations. The activation of PMS can effectively degrade the micropollutants BPA. The catalytic performance of the Fe-SA-NC was the best compared with the similar catalyst reported so far. Co\(\mathrm{N}_{4}\) SAs were designed and synthesized using the Fe-Co Prussian as the precursor (Fig. 8(c)) [90]. Fe-Co bimetal was encapsulated in nitrogen-doped graphene. The Co-SA was obtained by acid treatment (1 M \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(4\;\mathrm{h}\)). HRTEM, field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM), energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) mapping, and HAADF-STEM images were used to analyze the morphology of the as-prepared material. X-ray absorption spectroscopy, including the Co K-edge XANES, was applied to study the structural characterizations \(\left(\mathrm{CoN}_{4}\right)\). During this study, the lower reactivity of PMS onto the Co oxide or metallic Co proved the poisoning of the active sites caused by the strong bonding [90]. The PMS can be effectively activated during the following process: First, the PMS was adsorbed on the \(\mathrm{CoN}_{4}\) sites of the catalyst. Then a fast electron accumulation and depletion occurred with the production of a large amount of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [90]. Besides, the migration distance of the reaction sites was reduced (Fig. 8(d)). Li's group synthesized a novel Cu-SAs with an adjustable distance of copper atoms (denoted as the \(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Cu1-Cu1}}\)). PDS molecular can be activated during the Fenton-like process. The optimized distance of Cu was calculated to be \(0.5{-}0.6\ \mathrm{nm}\). This material was the most efficient catalyst for PDS activation. BPA can be completely degraded within only 6 min during the Fenton-like process [113]. The same research group conducted another study. An SA-Fe/CN was prepared by etching the \(\mathrm{Fe/CN@SiO}_{2}\) [114]. BPA was completely decomposed \((>90\%)\) within \(5\ \mathrm{min}\) by activating PMS in the Fenton-like reaction.
## 5.3 SAs in electron-Fenton degradation
Figure 9(a) shows that the \(\mathrm{Fe/Cu}\) bimetallic single-atom materials were designed [90]. The catalyst can be anchored onto the N-doped porous carbon, named FeCu-SA-NPC. The material was used as the modified cathode in the electro-Fenton process (Fig. 9(a)). Fe K-edge XANES spectra and HAADF-STEM characterized the atomically dispersed FeCu-SA-NPC catalyst. Based on the above-advanced techniques, the configuration of bimetallic sites was \(\mathrm{Fe-N}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu-N}_{4}\) respectively. A single-cell reactor was used for the electro-Fenton process. Oxygen was bubbled into the system continuously. The degradation follows the pseudo-first-order kinetics. The kinetic constants for the degradation of 4-CP follow the order of Fe-Cu-SA \((3.5\ \mathrm{h^{-1}})>\mathrm{Fe-SA}\) \((1.6\ \mathrm{h^{-1}})>\mathrm{Cu-SA}\) \((1.2\ \mathrm{h^{-1}})>\mathrm{NPC}\) without SA \((0.3\ \mathrm{h^{-1}})\). Thus the bimetallic single-atom materials show the highest activity for the degradation of 4-CP during the process of electro-Fenton. Results show that incorporating Fe and Cu can improve the ORR activity of the traditional carbon material (Fig. 9(b)). The co-doped metal Fe and Cu increase the ORR activity and facilitate the production of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in the electro-Fenton process. The degradation mechanism was studied using the density functional theory (DFT) calculation using the Vienna ab initio Simulation Package (VASP). The SA-Fe could significantly improve the ORR current density and decrease the ORR overpotential, while the SA-Cu \(\left(\mathrm{Cu-N}_{4}\right)\) is favorable for the adsorption of 4-CP with negative adsorption energy \((-0.34\ \mathrm{eV})\) [90]. The synergistic effect between the -OH generation and chlorination should be the primary mechanism in advanced water treatment.
Another Co-SAs were fabricated, supported by N-doped graphene [89]. This study found that the Co–N coordination sites can effectively activate the \(\mathrm{IO}_{4}^{-}\)in the ACAs. In comparison, the
\(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) cannot be used for the \(\mathrm{IO}_{4}\) activation. Figure 9(c) shows that 4-CP can be degraded completely within only \(12\ \mathrm{min}\). The conductive graphene support reduced the electron transport distance. Selective oxidation can be found in this study. Several different target compounds were selected as the model compound. Results show that the electro-donating group in the
chemical structure should be one of the mechanisms to cause the selectivity of this AOPs process (Fig. 9(d)). Electrochemical studies were conducted to prove that the electron-transfer-mediated non-radical process should be one of the primary mechanisms. Two adsorption configurations of graphene/CoN₄ (top) and graphene/CoN₂O₂ were studied in this work (Fig. 9(d)).
Theoretical calculations show that the isolated Co-N sites have higher activity than the structure's \(\mathrm{CoC_{4}}\) or the metal-free N/C sites.
A novel Cu-SAs was designed and further applied in the electroFenton system by Cui's research group [35]. Firstly, the single atom of Cu was successfully synthesized using the graphitic carbon nitride \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\right)\) as the support (Fig. 10(a)). EXAFS spectra and aberration-corrected HAADF-STEM image (Figs. 10(b) and 10(c)) were performed to confirm that the Cu atoms were located at the ring sites. Cu with single electron redox capacity was incorporated into the N-coordinating cavities of the graphitic carbon nitride. The as-prepared \(\mathrm{Cu-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) can be used as a heterogeneous Fenton catalyst. As shown in Fig. 10(d), during the Fenton process using the as-prepared SAs-Cu as the catalyst, over \(99\%\) of the organic pollutants (Rhodamine B, \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\)) in water can be effectively degraded within only 5 min reaction time. However, when \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) or \(\mathrm{CuO}\) was used as the catalyst, \(<40\%\) of Rhodamine B could be removed under the same reaction condition. On the other hand, the Cu-SAs were stable and could be used for ten cycles. The single atom Cu was preserved after reaction cycles, which can be confirmed by comparing the EXAFS spectra between the samples before and after the usages. In the second part, a 2-electron oxygen reduction \(\left(2\mathrm{e}^{-}\right.\) ORR) process occurred in a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) electrolyser on the gas diffusion electrodes (GDEs) as cathode (Figs. 10(e) and 10(f)). This GDE had hierarchical pores to facilitate the mass transport process. The \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was produced on the surface of the \(\mathrm{Cu-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) in the device [35]. Then it was decomposed in the Fenton-like reaction to produce a large amount of -OH, which can, in turn, degrade organic pollutants in water effectively over a long time (Figs. 10(e) and 10(f)). Additionally, this SA-based electrode in the electro-Fenton system is stable without obvious Cu leaching. This study addresses a strategy to solve two crucial challenges in the electro-Fenton process, including the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) activation simultaneously.
## 6 Conclusion
In summary, the substantial chemical requirement or energy consumption must be considered in the current AOPs process. Overcoming the limitation of high-energy consumption is crucial for applying AOPs in water treatment projects. Thus, developing novel materials to overcome the challenges in the advanced water treatment process is highly desired. ENMs as one kind of heterogeneous catalyst is a promising one. An increasing number of studies focus on the rational design, facile synthesis, and application of metal-organic framework, which derived carbon material for advanced water treatment. MOFs-based carbon has several advantages in environmental adsorption and catalysis because (a) MOFs derived carbon material can retain the original framework with high surface area and good stability in water; (b) predesigned MOFs can construct the adjustable active sites; (c) the regularly arranged metal ions in the MOFs are beneficial to avoid the aggregation of the metal/metal oxide nanoparticles and tend to be relatively ordered separated; (d) the hierarchical porous structures can increase the mass transfer process.
However, some limitations still need to be overcome because the catalytic performance needs to be improved. Compared to the MOFs-derived nanocluster or the traditional nanoparticles, SAs derived from MOFs have promising properties because of their maximum atomic utilization and ultrahigh catalytic properties. The unique electron configuration will facilitate the formation of a large amount of ROS. In addition, the reaction mechanism between the catalytic behavior and the active sites at the atomic level could be clearly explained. Although the application of this novel SA is still limited, the significant advantages of the SAs have been found in the area of the AOPs process (PMS/PDS/\(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) activation, electron/photo-Fenton reaction). In the future, more SAs should be rationally designed, and the reaction mechanism should be studied systematically.
